---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { src } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { VALUE } from '../../content/home';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Home insurance data publisher: who runs Home Insurance ZIP';
const description = 'Home Insurance ZIP is published by Radif Partners from the Treasury ZIP code file: premiums paid, nonrenewals and claims, 2018 to 2022. No policy sold, no form.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>Home Insurance ZIP: who publishes the data</h1>
    <p>Home Insurance ZIP publishes home insurance data from public sources only. {AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Search for the cost of homeowners insurance and you get averages that disagree by a thousand dollars, built from sample quotes for an invented house, followed by a form that asks for your phone number. In January 2025 the Treasury published what insurers actually wrote, ZIP code by ZIP code, for five years, along with how often they declined to renew. That file answers the question for real policies, and it deserved a plain lookup.</p>
    <p>{VALUE}</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public data and public rules. It is not an insurer, an agent, a broker or a lead generator, and it is not affiliated with the Treasury, the NAIC or any state insurance department. It earns no commission and carries no affiliate link.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the home insurance data come from</h2>
    <p>The Treasury's Federal Insurance Office workbook and report (<Fragment set:html={src('fio_data', 'workbook')} />, <Fragment set:html={src('fio_report', 'report')} />), the Bureau of Labor Statistics price indexes for homeowner's and tenants' insurance, the Census Bureau's ZIP to county file, federal escrow rules and the statutes cited on each page. Treasury did not validate the data in each ZIP code and does not endorse this site; its full disclaimer is on the <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a>.</p>
    <h2>What the site leaves out</h2>
    <p>It does not quote a premium, rank insurers or recommend one. It does not cover car insurance or flood insurance. Its figures end in 2022; later years are estimated with a national index and labeled. The <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how pages are written and corrected.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for corrections and sources. We answer questions about a figure or a rule, not about your own policy.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
